      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="notes">
        <tei:p>Written by Shlomo Shmulevitz and published in 1912, and again in 1916 the by Hebrew Publishing Company. The particular pandemic that inspired these lyrics may have been the “Russian Flu” (as the 1889-90 flu pandemic was sometimes named), or else the constant scourge of tuberculosis. At the time the Hebrew Publishing Company published these lyrics, the most recent worldwide plague was the 1915 Encephalitis lethargica pandemic. For more details, find this article at Tablet by Shalom Goldman.</tei:p>
        <tei:p>We are indebted to Sveta Kundish &amp; Daniel Kahn who brought these lyrics back to life, and to Yeva Lapske for their transcription. All on hand (Sveta, Daniel, Yeva Lapsker and Patrick Farrell) contributed to the translation. The text presented here was derived from the closed captions provided for the video on Youtube. Shalom Goldman properly credits Jane Peppler for initially unearthing this song for which she offered a romanized transcription and translation on her blog, Yiddish Penny Songs, in 2017. There she mentions that Mark Slobin discussed “Mentshn-Fresser” in his book, <tei:hi rend="italic">Tenement Songs</tei:hi> (1982) pp.148-150.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_32842_notes"/>
